1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:08,080
You just fell out of reality, into the Backrooms, and the first thing that can kill you here isn't a monster.

2
00:00:08,517 --> 00:00:09,717
It's thirst.

3
00:00:10,183 --> 00:00:16,983
No map. No exit sign. Just damp carpet, yellow walls, and a hum that never stops.

4
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:21,450
So I'm going to tell you how this goes, from your first step to your last.

5
00:00:21,917 --> 00:00:23,997
Because I'll be honest with you from the start.

6
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:25,970
You don't make it out.

7
00:00:26,417 --> 00:00:30,017
The only questions are how long you last, and what finally gets you.

8
00:00:30,483 --> 00:00:33,923
And the strangest part is that this place started with a real photo.

9
00:00:34,383 --> 00:00:39,903
It was taken in 2002, inside a store being renovated in Oshkosh, Wisconsin.

10
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:48,510
In 2019, someone posted it on 4chan with a short story about "six hundred million square miles of randomly segmented empty rooms."

11
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:52,790
Nobody knew where the photo came from until May 2024.

12
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:58,530
Since then, writers on the Backrooms Wiki have mapped that place, level by level.

13
00:00:58,983 --> 00:01:03,543
That wiki is our map today. Every rule in this story comes from it.

14
00:01:03,983 --> 00:01:06,143
Let's start with the hour you arrive.

15
00:01:07,933 --> 00:01:11,533
It happens the way it often happens: by accident.

16
00:01:11,983 --> 00:01:15,983
You trip in a dark hallway at home, and instead of hitting the floor, you go through it.

17
00:01:16,450 --> 00:01:21,010
Level 0. The wiki calls it Threshold. Most people know it as the Lobby.

18
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:26,250
Yellow wallpaper. Fluorescent lights. And carpet that is always wet.

19
00:01:26,717 --> 00:01:31,197
The smell is the first thing that tells you something is wrong. Old, soaked carpet.

20
00:01:31,650 --> 00:01:33,730
The second thing is the sound.

21
00:01:34,183 --> 00:01:38,743
The lights hum, and the hum rises and falls, but it never stops.

22
00:01:39,183 --> 00:01:40,943
That part is real, by the way.

23
00:01:41,383 --> 00:01:45,783
Old fluorescent lights flicker about a hundred times a second, too fast for you to see.

24
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:53,370
In a 1989 study in British offices, switching to lights that didn't flicker cut headaches and eyestrain by more than half.

25
00:01:53,817 --> 00:01:56,537
So within an hour, your head hurts.

26
00:01:56,983 --> 00:01:59,703
You call out. Nobody answers.

27
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:02,630
And here's the first rule of Level 0.

28
00:02:03,083 --> 00:02:07,483
Even if someone had fallen in right beside you, you would never find each other.

29
00:02:07,950 --> 00:02:09,950
You're alone. Completely.

30
00:02:10,417 --> 00:02:11,777
So you walk.

31
00:02:12,217 --> 00:02:16,697
Every room looks like the back of an office, or a store that was never finished.

32
00:02:17,150 --> 00:02:20,510
Psychologists have a name for why that feels so wrong.

33
00:02:20,950 --> 00:02:29,670
In 2022, a study found that places feel most uncanny when they're almost normal, with something in their structure slightly off.

34
00:02:30,117 --> 00:02:35,957
Level 0 is exactly that. Familiar, and wrong, in every direction.

35
00:02:37,767 --> 00:02:40,567
By hour nine, you notice something worse.

36
00:02:41,017 --> 00:02:45,257
You've seen this room before. That stain. That corner.

37
00:02:45,717 --> 00:02:47,637
You're walking in circles.

38
00:02:48,083 --> 00:02:50,563
That isn't just the Backrooms. That's you.

39
00:02:51,017 --> 00:02:57,817
In 2009, German researchers sent people walking for hours through a forest and across the Sahara, tracked by GPS.

40
00:02:58,283 --> 00:03:02,523
When they could see the sun, they drifted, but kept going straight.

41
00:03:02,983 --> 00:03:05,543
When clouds covered it, they walked in circles.

42
00:03:05,983 --> 00:03:10,223
Blindfolded, some walked circles less than twenty meters wide.

43
00:03:10,683 --> 00:03:12,603
In Level 0, there is no sun.

44
00:03:13,050 --> 00:03:17,210
And there's something else. The wiki calls it the Peripheral Shift.

45
00:03:17,650 --> 00:03:24,450
When you're not looking, the rooms rearrange themselves, sometimes erasing days of hallways you've already walked.

46
00:03:24,917 --> 00:03:27,397
So the map in your head is useless.

47
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:29,610
Then there's the thirst.

48
00:03:30,050 --> 00:03:34,530
The carpet is wet, but what soaks it is nothing you should drink.

49
00:03:34,983 --> 00:03:38,823
Some of it is salt water. Some of it is formaldehyde.

50
00:03:39,283 --> 00:03:47,443
Real research shows that losing just one or two percent of your body weight in water already hurts your mood and focus, and gives you headaches.

51
00:03:47,883 --> 00:03:55,323
And in Level 0, the wiki says, most lives end exactly this way: dehydration, or starvation.

52
00:03:55,783 --> 00:03:58,903
Then, somewhere to your left, a wall flickers.

53
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:06,060
You don't know how long you've been here. Your phone died on day one. Or what you think was day one.

54
00:04:06,517 --> 00:04:15,317
In 1962, a French scientist named Michel Siffre lived alone in a cave for about two months, with no clock and no sunlight.

55
00:04:15,783 --> 00:04:19,223
When they told him it was over, he thought it was the twentieth of August.

56
00:04:19,683 --> 00:04:22,723
It was the middle of September. He had lost almost a month.

57
00:04:23,183 --> 00:04:26,143
Keep that in mind. Your count is already wrong.

58
00:04:26,583 --> 00:04:30,183
The flickering wall is the way out. You throw yourself through it.

59
00:04:30,650 --> 00:04:36,090
If you want the next story like this one, that's what the subscribe button is for. Now, Level 1.

60
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:41,260
Level 1. The wiki calls it the Habitable Zone.

61
00:04:41,717 --> 00:04:48,837
It's an endless underground parking garage crossed with an abandoned warehouse. Concrete, pillars, pipes.

62
00:04:49,283 --> 00:04:54,563
And it has the two things Level 0 didn't: supplies, and people.

63
00:04:55,017 --> 00:04:59,497
Wooden crates appear out of nowhere, often right behind your back.

64
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:03,630
Inside: food, clothes, tools. And metal bottles.

65
00:05:04,083 --> 00:05:05,603
Almond Water.

66
00:05:06,050 --> 00:05:13,570
According to the wiki, one full bottle is worth a healthy six-hundred-calorie meal. Three a day keep you alive.

67
00:05:14,017 --> 00:05:18,497
One rule: never drink from a bottle that isn't full. Someone else had it first.

68
00:05:18,950 --> 00:05:24,070
You sleep in one of the small rooms off the corridors, with furniture pushed against the door.

69
00:05:24,517 --> 00:05:30,517
But you never lock it. Locks here have a way of disappearing, and people have died trapped inside.

70
00:05:30,983 --> 00:05:34,183
On what you think is day five, the lights go out.

71
00:05:34,650 --> 00:05:39,850
The wiki calls it Flickering, and says it might be one of the deadliest things in the Backrooms.

72
00:05:40,317 --> 00:05:42,717
In the dark, things come out.

73
00:05:43,183 --> 00:05:46,383
The rule is simple: don't turn on your flashlight.

74
00:05:46,850 --> 00:05:51,490
Being the only light in the area, the wiki says, leads to a quick death.

75
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:54,190
So you sit in the dark, and you listen.

76
00:05:54,650 --> 00:05:57,450
Something walks past your door. Then it stops.

77
00:05:57,917 --> 00:05:59,997
And a human voice says your name.

78
00:06:00,450 --> 00:06:05,010
Hold on to that moment. This exact trick comes back at the end of your story.

79
00:06:05,450 --> 00:06:07,610
You don't answer. The lights come back.

80
00:06:08,050 --> 00:06:10,930
In the morning, you find out what it was.

81
00:06:11,383 --> 00:06:17,383
The wiki calls them Skin-Stealers: tall, pale things that wear the skin of the people they catch.

82
00:06:17,850 --> 00:06:20,650
They copy voices without understanding the words.

83
00:06:21,117 --> 00:06:27,757
Survivors have a rhyme for them: "Blood runs red, they're not dead. Blood runs clear, don't get near."

84
00:06:28,217 --> 00:06:32,857
The people who teach you that rhyme wear lime-yellow, with an eagle on the shoulder.

85
00:06:33,317 --> 00:06:36,437
The M.E.G. The Major Explorer Group.

86
00:06:36,883 --> 00:06:38,243
You've been found.

87
00:06:40,033 --> 00:06:44,833
Base Alpha is the oldest M.E.G. base, built in the corridors of Level 1.

88
00:06:45,283 --> 00:06:52,003
Newcomers take a test, and then get a job: exploration, archives, research or maintenance.

89
00:06:52,450 --> 00:06:55,330
You end up in exploration. Of course you do.

90
00:06:55,783 --> 00:06:59,463
There's a diner. People trade Almond Water like money.

91
00:06:59,917 --> 00:07:05,597
For a few weeks, life is almost normal. Shifts, meals, sleep.

92
00:07:06,050 --> 00:07:13,330
Your partner on the team is a woman who's been down here for six years. She teaches you how to hold a rifle without shaking.

93
00:07:13,783 --> 00:07:17,303
And she teaches you the rules the veterans never joke about.

94
00:07:17,750 --> 00:07:23,830
One: if you see party decorations, or a poster that says "Time 4 Fun", turn around.

95
00:07:24,283 --> 00:07:27,083
Two: never trust a voice. Trust blood.

96
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:36,510
At the gate of Base Gamma, the big M.E.G. base on Level 3, a guard pricks the finger of everyone who walks in, to check that their blood is red.

97
00:07:36,950 --> 00:07:42,950
The wiki says no exceptions are ever made. Not even for the people who run the M.E.G.

98
00:07:43,417 --> 00:07:47,657
Three: Level 3 is the one place nobody jokes about at all.

99
00:07:48,117 --> 00:07:52,197
Even the M.E.G. calls traveling there "extremely ill-advised."

100
00:07:52,650 --> 00:07:58,090
Twenty percent of its entire budget goes to one thing: keeping Base Gamma alive.

101
00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:05,260
Months pass. Your count says day two hundred. You don't trust it anymore.

102
00:08:05,717 --> 00:08:12,277
Then your team gets its first long expedition: carry medicine and data from Base Alpha to Base Gamma.

103
00:08:12,717 --> 00:08:15,677
Two levels. Level 2, then Level 3.

104
00:08:16,117 --> 00:08:19,957
Eight of you. Rifles, flares, and a crate of supplies.

105
00:08:20,417 --> 00:08:26,977
Level 2 first. Maintenance tunnels. Pipes everywhere. Machines humming with no purpose.

106
00:08:27,417 --> 00:08:30,377
Every turn is exactly forty-five degrees.

107
00:08:30,817 --> 00:08:35,777
The lights are wired together, so when one fails, a whole grid of them goes dark.

108
00:08:36,217 --> 00:08:43,337
In the dark parts, there are Smilers. You never see a body. Just a long smile full of teeth, and two white eyes.

109
00:08:43,783 --> 00:08:49,863
So you do what you were taught. Light off. Eye contact. Walk away slowly.

110
00:08:50,317 --> 00:08:56,717
At every corner, you go silent, because of the Clumps: piles of limbs that wait in tight corridors. Within eight feet, they reach.

111
00:08:57,183 --> 00:09:01,823
Nobody dies on Level 2. For a moment, you think the hard part is over.

112
00:09:02,283 --> 00:09:03,723
Then you reach the door.

113
00:09:04,183 --> 00:09:11,863
Most of the doors from Level 2 into Level 3 are barricaded, or covered in warning signs. This one has both. And a gap.

114
00:09:12,317 --> 00:09:16,877
Psychologists say walking through a doorway makes people forget what they were just holding in mind.

115
00:09:17,317 --> 00:09:22,357
Remember that. On the other side, forgetting one small rule is all it takes.

116
00:09:24,167 --> 00:09:26,487
Level 3. The Electrical Station.

117
00:09:26,950 --> 00:09:34,390
Brick walls in mismatched colors. Dusty concrete. Weak fluorescent lights. Cables and fuse boxes everywhere.

118
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:40,290
Pipes full of a thick black liquid. Some corridors are barely wider than your shoulders.

119
00:09:40,750 --> 00:09:45,870
And metal bars across most of the hallways. Bars nobody has ever managed to break.

120
00:09:46,317 --> 00:09:51,517
The danger here isn't one monster. It's what the monsters do together.

121
00:09:51,983 --> 00:09:59,903
The wiki says the entities on Level 3 attack in "military-esque brigades", with careful planning, and a better map of the level than yours.

122
00:10:00,350 --> 00:10:04,750
Seeing one entity wandering by itself almost never happens.

123
00:10:05,217 --> 00:10:10,097
And sometimes, those brigades lead a wanderer, on purpose, into a dead end.

124
00:10:10,550 --> 00:10:14,710
Two hours in, you meet a man in a hooded coat and a mask.

125
00:10:15,150 --> 00:10:20,430
He says he's lost. He has a thick accent. He's limping. He asks for water.

126
00:10:20,883 --> 00:10:25,283
You're tired. He's human. Nobody asks to see his blood.

127
00:10:25,750 --> 00:10:31,110
A hundred yards later, there's a dim light at the end of the corridor. No lamp. No source.

128
00:10:31,550 --> 00:10:38,030
The wiki has a line about those lights: Smilers with their eyes and lips shut, standing guard for the brigades.

129
00:10:38,483 --> 00:10:40,803
Someone points a flashlight at it.

130
00:10:41,250 --> 00:10:43,010
The smile opens.

131
00:10:43,450 --> 00:10:48,010
Behind you, the man in the coat pulls at his own face, and the skin comes loose.

132
00:10:48,450 --> 00:10:52,850
Something leaps from the dark at the back of the line. A Hound.

133
00:10:53,317 --> 00:10:59,717
The wiki says the Hounds here have mastered surprise: they hide, and pounce from behind.

134
00:11:00,183 --> 00:11:04,903
The last man in your line is gone before anyone can turn around.

135
00:11:05,350 --> 00:11:10,390
So you run. Eight of you, then seven, down the only hallway that's open.

136
00:11:10,850 --> 00:11:14,210
Behind you, the barking gets faster. More excited.

137
00:11:14,650 --> 00:11:20,730
The wiki explains that too. On Level 3, the Hounds bark faster when their prey turns toward a dead end.

138
00:11:21,183 --> 00:11:24,623
You turn left. The barking turns into a howl.

139
00:11:26,433 --> 00:11:28,993
The corridor ends in metal bars.

140
00:11:29,450 --> 00:11:33,850
And through the bars, maybe forty yards away, you can see the lights of Base Gamma.

141
00:11:34,317 --> 00:11:39,917
Guards. Sandbags. A gate. A man with a needle, ready to prick your finger.

142
00:11:40,383 --> 00:11:44,863
If anyone had pricked one finger two hours ago, you'd be standing over there.

143
00:11:45,317 --> 00:11:47,957
Your team turns around and fights.

144
00:11:48,417 --> 00:11:54,497
Clumps eight feet tall, holding weapons. Facelings with brick shields and blades.

145
00:11:54,950 --> 00:12:01,350
And somewhere behind them, giving orders, a Death Rat. The wiki calls them the leaders of these operations.

146
00:12:01,817 --> 00:12:08,297
Rifles. Flares. Your partner throws Firesalt, and for a moment the corridor burns white.

147
00:12:08,750 --> 00:12:10,670
Then the Deathmoths come in.

148
00:12:11,117 --> 00:12:15,677
The wiki says a swarm goes for the mouth and the lungs, and can kill in a matter of seconds.

149
00:12:16,117 --> 00:12:19,237
Your partner goes down first. You hear her stop breathing.

150
00:12:19,683 --> 00:12:21,843
Then the others. One by one.

151
00:12:22,283 --> 00:12:25,803
You're the last one, back against the bars, out of ammunition.

152
00:12:26,250 --> 00:12:29,050
The Hounds on Level 3 don't kill quickly.

153
00:12:29,517 --> 00:12:33,597
The wiki says they try to make it as slow and as painful as possible.

154
00:12:34,050 --> 00:12:39,570
One bites the back of your neck. Not enough to kill you. Just enough so you can't move.

155
00:12:40,017 --> 00:12:43,057
You open your mouth to scream for the guards at the gate.

156
00:12:43,517 --> 00:12:46,637
The second Hound takes your voice before you can.

157
00:12:47,083 --> 00:12:50,763
The lights of Base Gamma are still on. Forty yards.

158
00:12:52,567 --> 00:12:55,127
The M.E.G. keeps a record of every wanderer.

159
00:12:55,583 --> 00:13:01,263
Yours is short. Expedition to Base Gamma. Eight members. None arrived.

160
00:13:01,717 --> 00:13:05,477
Your count said you'd been in the Backrooms for about two hundred days.

161
00:13:05,917 --> 00:13:09,597
By M.E.G. time, it was closer to four hundred.

162
00:13:10,050 --> 00:13:15,010
The guards at Base Gamma reported barking that night, very close to the bars.

163
00:13:15,450 --> 00:13:17,130
Nobody heard a scream.

164
00:13:17,583 --> 00:13:20,463
Base Gamma still checks every finger at the gate.

165
00:13:20,917 --> 00:13:24,837
And on Level 3, a few weeks later, a patrol meets a man in a hooded coat.

166
00:13:25,283 --> 00:13:28,723
He's lost. He's limping. He asks for water.

167
00:13:29,183 --> 00:13:30,463
He has your voice.
